# mlibrary: build prompt 1 (foundation, two heroes, footer, EN/MN)

You are building **mlibrary**, a small book-archive website, in English and Mongolian.
This prompt has two parts:

1. **The master layer** (this part). It says what the site is, how the pages connect, what changes from the source designs, and how Mongolian must behave.
2. **Three source designs** (Appendix A, B, C), pasted verbatim. They define every pixel, timing, easing, asset and animation of the hero sections and the footer.

## How to read the appendices

- The appendices are law for **geometry, sizes, timing, easing, colours, assets, crop boxes, animation, and responsive behaviour**. Do not "improve" them. Do not change an easing, a duration, a size or a breakpoint.
- This master layer wins only where it says so, and only for these things: brand, copy, data, language, routes, navigation, fonts for Cyrillic, how the code is organised, and the places listed under "Overrides" below.
- Ignore the meta sentences in the appendices about "one file", "no framework", "no JS", "embed fonts as base64", "create exactly one file" and "document title". They described how the templates were exported. Here the site is a normal multi-route app (see "Code structure").
- The artwork is **not changed**. The twelve spine and cover images keep the text drawn into them (an English title, "A LEON VASARI FILM", a "VHS" badge). Do not try to cover, crop out or redraw that text. Only text that lives in HTML changes.

## Stack and code quality

- React 19 + Vite + plain JavaScript (`.jsx` / `.js`; only `server.ts` is TypeScript), `react-router-dom` for routes, an Express server (`server.ts`) for the API, `node:sqlite` locally and plain PostgreSQL (`pg`) through `DATABASE_URL` in production. No Firebase or other backend-as-a-service (see "Database").
- The code must read like it was written by a careful human engineer who will hand it to a full-stack team: clear camelCase names, one responsibility per file, short comments only where the reason is not obvious, no leftover prompt wording, no "as an AI", no dead code, no console noise.
- No secrets in the client. Every setting comes from environment variables and is listed in `.env.example`.

### Code structure

```
src/
  app/            App.jsx, routes.jsx, ShellLayout.jsx, PageTransition.jsx
  components/
    shell/        SiteHeader.jsx, MenuSheet.jsx, LanguageToggle.jsx, CustomCursor.jsx, PaperGrain.jsx
    footer/       SiteFooter.jsx, footer.css
  features/
    curvedArchive/  CurvedArchive.jsx, engine/*.js, curvedArchive.css   (Appendix B)
    flatShelf/      FlatShelf.jsx,   engine/*.js, flatShelf.css         (Appendix A)
  content/        books.js, assets.js (SHEET and CROP tables), decades.js
  i18n/           index.js, en.js, mn.js, useLocale.js
  pages/          HomePage, BooksPage, AboutPage, JournalPage, ArticlePage, ContactPage, LegalPage, NotFoundPage
  styles/         tokens.css, base.css
server.ts
functions/        api/*.js, repository/{sqliteRepo,pgRepo,index}.js
db/               schema.postgres.sql, schema.sqlite.sql
docs/             DATA.md
```

**Hero engines stay imperative.** Port each hero's vanilla JavaScript into plain ES modules under `engine/` (for example `createCurvedArchive(container, options)` returning `{ destroy, setLocale, goTo, openBook, closeBook }`). The React component only mounts the engine in `useEffect`, passes the data, and calls `destroy()` on unmount. Do not rewrite the animation loop with React state. The maths, the `requestAnimationFrame` loop, the Web Animations calls and every number stay exactly as in the appendices.

**Scoping.** The heroes are full-screen, `position: fixed` apps. Scope their rules under a wrapper class (`.curved-archive`, `.flat-shelf`). Rules that touch `html` or `body` (hidden overflow, hidden native cursor) apply only while a hero route is mounted, through a `hero-active` class on `<body>`. Content pages scroll normally.

